// Package sys is Layer 0 of napi-go: 1:1 bindings for Node-API (N-API) functions for writing
// native Node.js addons (.node) in Go.
//
// Most of this package is generated by internal/gen from the official headers
// (include/, node-api-headers): every C function napi_xxx / node_api_xxx has a Go wrapper with
// the same parameter order, e.g. napi_create_int32 → CreateInt32. For an idiomatic Go API use
// Layer 1 (package napi); sys is intended for callers that need direct access to the raw ABI.
//
// # Wrapper Conventions
//
//   - Outputs: C `T*` output parameters remain pointers in Go, but the C wrapper returns them
//     by value so no Go pointer crosses cgo and no heap allocations occur. A nil pointer passes
//     NULL (some functions change behavior when given NULL, e.g. napi_wrap without result).
//     Outputs are written only when the returned status is StatusOK.
//   - Opaque data: `void*` values retained by Node-API (data, hint, context, arg, native_object)
//     are represented as uintptr. By cgo rules they must not be unpinned Go pointers: use an
//     index, a runtime/cgo.Handle, or C-allocated memory.
//   - Input buffers and arrays (`const char*`, `const napi_value*`, ...) are Go pointers valid
//     only for the duration of the call. Structs passed by pointer (PropertyDescriptor) must not
//     contain Go pointers unless pinned with runtime.Pinner.
//   - Missing functions: all functions are resolved at runtime from the host process. If the
//     Node.js binary loading the addon does not export a function, its wrapper returns
//     StatusUnavailable; Available(Fn...) reports availability in advance.
//
// # Threading
//
// Env, Value, and other handles are valid only on the env's JavaScript thread and, for Values,
// only within the active callback or handle scope. From arbitrary goroutines only Node-API's
// thread-safe functions may be called: CallThreadsafeFunction, AcquireThreadsafeFunction,
// ReleaseThreadsafeFunction, and GetThreadsafeFunctionContext.
//
// # Entries into Go
//
// Node-API receives C function pointers (CallbackTrampoline, FinalizeTrampoline, ...) that enter
// Go through handlers registered with SetCallbackHandler, SetFinalizeHandler, etc. Module
// initialization is handled by SetModuleInitHandler. Handlers should be registered in init().
// A panic reaching sys is converted into a JS Error with code "GO_PANIC" wherever an exception
// can be thrown; elsewhere (finalizers, async work execute, cleanup hooks) it terminates the
// process, matching an unrecovered Go panic.
//
// # Declared Node-API Version
//
// The module reports DeclaredVersion to Node.js via node_api_module_get_api_version_v1.
// The default is 8; build tags napi_go_v9, napi_go_v10, and napi_go_experimental raise it.
// Functions up to the declared version must be present in the host or module loading fails with
// ERR_NAPI_GO_MISSING_FUNCTIONS; newer functions remain optional. The build tag
// napi_go_test_version_mismatch, reserved for tests, declares an unsupported version.
//
// Without a ModuleInitHandler (SetModuleInitHandler), module loading fails with
// ERR_NAPI_GO_NO_HANDLER, matching Node.js's behavior for a module with no entry point.
//
// # Platform Limitations
//
//   - macOS x64: all Go runtimes in a process share the TLS slot for g, so only one Go addon per
//     process is supported. A second addon is rejected with ERR_NAPI_GO_FOREIGN_RUNTIME instead
//     of corrupting memory.
//   - macOS: after Go runtime initialization, sys restores SA_RESETHAND on Node's SIGINT/SIGTERM
//     handlers, which the Go runtime strips (otherwise the process hangs on signal exit).
//   - musl (Alpine): not supported by current Go releases for c-shared builds.
package sys

//go:generate go run ./internal/gen
